      "id": "C50",
      "text": "Inclusion in an NLM database does not imply endorsement of, or agreement with, the contents by NLM or the National Institutes of Health.",
      "type": "factual",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S1",
      ],
      "id": "C51",
      "text": "Learn more: PMC Disclaimer | PMC Copyright Notice J Cell Mol Med.",
      "type": "factual",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S1",
      ],
      "id": "C52",
      "text": "2026 Apr 25;30(8):e71159.",
      "type": "numeric",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S1",
      ],
      "id": "C53",
      "text": "doi: 10.1111/jcmm.71159 Advances in CRISPR Base Editing: From Molecular Evolution to Therapeutic Applications in Genomic Medicine Melike Aliciaslan Melike Aliciaslan 1 Department of Gene and Cell Therapy, Faculty of Medicine, Akdeniz University, Antalya, Turkey Find articles by Melike Aliciaslan 1, Ezgi Erbasan Ezgi Erbasan 1 Department of Gene and Cell Therapy, Faculty of Medicine, Akdeniz University, Antalya, Turkey Find articles by Ezgi Erbasan 1,  [...] uent cellular repair (NHEJ or HDR) leads to gene editing outcomes.",
      "type": "numeric",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S1",
      ],
      "id": "C54",
      "text": "(D) Base Editors (BEs): Derived from the CRISPR system, Base Editors enable precise single‐base changes without inducing DSBs [5].",
      "type": "numeric",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S1",
      ],
      "id": "C55",
      "text": "They consist of a catalytically impaired Cas protein (either a nickase, nCas9, which cuts only one strand, or a deactivated Cas9, dCas9, which doesn't cut at all) fused to a DNA deaminase enzyme (e.g., cytidine deaminase for C•G to T•A edits or adenine deaminase for A•T to G•C edits).",
      "type": "numeric",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S1",
      ],
      "id": "C56",
      "text": "Guided by an sgRNA, the editor binds to the target DNA, and the deaminase directly converts a target base within a specific editing window.",
      "type": "factual",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S1",
      ],
      "id": "C57",
      "text": "A nick on the non‐edited strand often enhances the efficiency of incorporating the edit during DNA repair.",
      "type": "factual",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S1",
      ],
      "id": "C58",
      "text": "(E) Prime Editors (PEs): A versatile ‘search‐and‐replace’ genome editing tech [...] DR efficiency), led researchers to invent new, more accurate gene editing tools.",
      "type": "factual",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S1",
      ],
      "id": "C59",
      "text": "Base editing represents a significant leap forward, enabling targeted single‐nucleotide conversions directly on the DNA without DSBs or donor templates.",
      "type": "factual",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S1",
      ],
      "id": "C60",
      "text": "The core technology involves fusing catalytically dead or nickase Cas proteins to DNA deaminase enzymes.",
      "type": "factual",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S1",
      ],
      "id": "C61",
      "text": "Cytosine base editors (CBEs) convert C•G to T•A pairs, while adenine base editors (ABEs) change A•T to G•C.",
      "type": "factual",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S1",
      ],
      "id": "C62",
      "text": "These editors exploit the deaminase function within the R‐loop structure formed by Cas binding and co‐opt endogenous DNA repair mechanisms for precision.",
      "type": "factual",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S1",
      ],
      "id": "C63",
      "text": "While offering improved efficiency and editing precision, base editing faces persistent challenges, such as off‐target effects, bystander edits, delivery and ethical concerns.",
      "type": "factual",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S1",
      ],
      "id": "C64",
      "text": "Continuous engineering efforts",
      "type": "factual",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S2",
      ],
      "id": "C65",
      "text": "nisms; therefore, there is a need to develop base editors that can simultaneously achieve C-to-T and A-to-G substitutions at the targeting site.",
      "type": "causation",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S2",
      ],
      "id": "C66",
      "text": "In this study, a novel fusion adenine and cytosine base editor (ACBE) was generated by fusing a heterodimer of TadA (ecTadAWT/*) and an activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID) to the N- and C-terminals of Cas9 nickase (nCas9), respectively.",
      "type": "numeric",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S2",
      ],
      "id": "C67",
      "text": "ACBE could simultaneously induce C-to-T and A-to-G base editing at the same target site, which were verified in HEK293-EGFP reporter cell line and 45 endogenous gene loci of HEK293 cells.",
      "type": "numeric",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S2",
      ],
      "id": "C68",
      "text": "Moreover, the ACBE could accomplish simultaneous point mutations of C-to-T and A-to-G in primary somatic cells (mouse embryonic fibroblasts and porcine fetal fibroblasts) in an applicable efficiency.",
      "type": "factual",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S2",
      ],
      "id": "C69",
      "text": "Furthermore, the spacer length [...] in this article, unless otherwise stated in a credit line to the data.",
      "type": "factual",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S2",
      ],
      "id": "C70",
      "text": "Many favorable traits of crops and livestock and human genetic diseases arise from multiple single nucleotide polymorphisms or multiple point mutations with heterogeneous base substitutions at the same locus.",
      "type": "factual",
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        "S2",
      ],
      "id": "C71",
      "text": "Current cytosine or adenine base editors can only accomplish C-to-T (G-to-A) or A-to-G (T-to-C) substitutions in the windows of target genomic sites of organisms; therefore, there is a need to develop base editors that can simultaneously achieve C-to-T and A-to-G substitutions at the targeting site.",
      "type": "causation",
    },
    {
